#!/usr/bin/perl
use strict;
use warnings;

use Mail::DMARC;
use Mail::DMARC::HTTP;

my $dmarc = Mail::DMARC->new();
$dmarc->is_public_suffix('tnpi.net');
my $report = $dmarc->report;
my $http   = Mail::DMARC::HTTP->new;
$http->dmarc_httpd($report);
exit;

__END__

=pod

=head1 NAME

dmarc_httpd: a web server for DMARC validation and report viewing

=head1 SYNOPSIS

A HTTP interface for:

=over 4

=item * local DMARC reports

=item * DMARC validator service

=back

Start the HTTP server:

    dmarc_httpd

Connect with a web browser to L<http://localhost:8080|http://localhost:8080/>.

=head1 DESCRIPTION

The HTTP server handles these types of requests:

=over 4

=item * /

Serves the report viewer from the perl share directory of the Mail::DMARC
module: one HTML file, one stylesheet and a handful of JavaScript modules.
They have no external dependencies and are loaded from this server alone, so
the viewer works on a host with no route to the internet.

=item * /dmarc/json/validate - DMARC validation requests

Accepts a JSON encoded HTTP POST request. Validates the request, performs a
DMARC validation and returns a JSON encoded result object. This is the API
for non-perl applications to utilize Mail::DMARC.

See the dmarc_http_client app for a usage example.

=item * /dmarc/json/report

Accepts AJAX requests from the browser and returns JSON encoded DMARC reports.

=item * /dmarc/json/row

Accepts AJAX requests from the browser and returns JSON encoded DMARC report rows.

=item * /dmarc/json/domains

Returns every domain reports have been received for, with a report count and
the epoch bounds of the reporting windows seen.

=item * /dmarc/json/summary

Returns message volume for a reporting window, broken down by DMARC alignment
and by disposition, alongside the same figures for the equal length window
immediately before it.

=item * /dmarc/json/timeseries

Returns the same breakdown as the summary, one row per UTC day. A report whose
window straddles a day boundary is attributed to the day its window begins.

=item * /dmarc/json/sources

Returns one row per sending IP, ranked by the volume of mail that failed
DMARC, with the disposition reasons reporters supplied. Each source is bucketed
as C<aligned> (nothing failed), C<forwarded> (the failures are accounted for by
forwarding or mailing list reasons), or C<failing> (needs attention).

=item * /dmarc/json/source

Returns the detail behind one source IP: volume per header From domain,
disposition and alignment, and the SPF domains and DKIM domains and selectors
the source presented. Buckets the source as C<aligned>, C<broken> (it presented
authentication that did not pass or align) or C<unauthenticated> (it presented
none), which is the distinction between a misconfigured sender and a spoof.

=back

A store also holds the reports this installation is preparing to send, which
are about other people's domains as seen here, rather than about your domains
as seen by others. Blending the two answers neither question, so the summary,
timeseries, sources and domain views count only reports received from others.
Pass C<reports=outgoing> for the queue alone or C<reports=all> for both. The
report list is an audit trail and shows both unless asked otherwise. The two
are told apart by the report's author, so C<org_name> in the [organization]
section of mail-dmarc.ini must match the name this host reports under.

The aggregate views above accept these query parameters. C<since> and C<until>
are epoch seconds compared against the report window start, and default to the
last 30 days. C<from_domain> and C<author> restrict results to one From domain
or one reporting organization. C<source_ip> selects a single source and is
required by /dmarc/json/source. C<sources> also accepts C<start>, C<length>
and C<sort_col> (C<messages> to rank by total volume instead of failures).
Volumes are weighted by the message count in each report record, and all times
in these responses are epoch seconds. A record with no count is one message:
the local evaluation path writes a row per message and fills the count in when
the report is aggregated for sending.

An implementation that uses the http validation service is the included <dmarc_http_client> and another is the dmarc plugin in the <Haraka mail server|http://haraka.github.io>.

The web interface is implemented almost entirely in JavaScript, and opens on
the question an operator actually has: what share of this domain's mail
authenticates, and which sending sources are breaking the rest. It has three
views.

B<Overview> states the share of mail that would survive C<p=reject>, breaks the
volume down by whether SPF, DKIM, both or neither aligned, compares the window
against the one before it, and charts daily volume by alignment.

B<Sources> lists sending IPs ranked by the volume of mail they are failing,
each assessed as C<aligned>, C<forwarded> or C<failing>. Expanding a source
shows the SPF domains and the DKIM domains and selectors it presented, which
is what separates a misconfigured sender from one that authenticates nothing
at all. A source's detail has its own link, so one offending sender can be
pasted into a ticket.

B<Reports> is the audit trail, one row per aggregate report received, expanding
to the rows it contained along with any override reasons the receiver supplied.

The selected domain and reporting window apply to every view, and all view
state is kept in the URL.

Web server settings are in the [http] and [https] sections of mail-dmarc.ini.

Ticking "Resolve hostnames" looks up the PTR record of each source IP on
display and shows the hostname beneath the address. It is off by default
because it sends those source IPs to a third party DNS-over-HTTPS resolver. Cloudflare and Google are offered in the adjacent menu, and "Other"
accepts any DoH server that answers JSON queries with CORS headers, including
your own. A hand-entered resolver is checked as you type, with a single query
for a fixed public name, and is only adopted once it answers. Both settings
are remembered per browser.

The lookups happen in the browser rather than the server, so the many source
IPs with no PTR record can't stall dmarc_httpd.

Databases created before Mail::DMARC 2.2 lack indexes that the aggregate views
need; without them every lookup by report id scans the whole report_record
table. F<share/mail_dmarc_indexes.*> adds them to an existing database, one
file per supported engine.

